          %h2#GroupCompletedConfigurationSpaces Group completed configuration spaces of unlabeled points
          %p
            We briefly review
            %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"} Okuyama’s
            configuration-spaces of “charged strings” as models for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            of plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces+of+points"} configuration spaces of points
            without labels (or rather: with labels in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-sphere"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ChargedParticlesAndStrings"} Charged particles and strings
          %p
            Using this, we observe that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loops"} based loops
            in Okuyama’s configuration space of points in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            are essentially identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"} framed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+link"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/links"} links
            whose class in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq \pi^3(S^2) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
            is twice their total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linking+number"} linking number
            plus their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
            (thus reproving the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+theorem"} Pontrjagin theorem
            in these dimensions):
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#LoopsOfChargedStrings"} Charged string loops as framed links
          %h3#ChargedParticlesAndStrings Charged particles and strings
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
            for the “plain”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of
            unlabeled and unordered points in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"}> Euclidean space
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            This space naturally carries the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} abelian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+monoid"} topological monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint"} disjoint
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Segal73"}> Segal 1973, p. 215
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p Here we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Ω
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊔
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\Omega B_{\sqcup} Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            of the configuration space with respect to this partial operation of disjoint union.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Segal73"} Segal 1973, Thm. 1
            says, in particular, that this group completed configuration space is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalent
            to the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iterated+loop+space"} iterated loop space
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            (the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle+space"} cocycle space
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi cpt
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n_{cpt}
            of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
          #eq:SegalTheorem1.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{G} Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\Omega^n S^n
                  \\,,
          %p This motivates asking for more explicit descriptions of the group completed configuration space of points, itself as a configuration space, now of objects somewhat richer than plain points.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OkuyamaRelations-240714.jpg", :width => "300px"}/
          %p
            Since the group completion must introduce the existence of a kind of “anti-points” much in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antiparticles"} antiparticles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
            should be equivalent to the configuration space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Conf
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf^{\pm}(\mathbb{R}^n)
            of
            %em charged
            points, where each point in a configuration carries a label in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\pm 1\}
            and where equal-charged points cannot coincide (they “repel”) while pairs of oppositely charged points may coincide in which case they annihilate &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#McDuff75"}> McDuff 1975, p. 94
            \&amp;rbrack;. However, this is
            %em not
            the case:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Conf
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf^{\pm}(\mathbb{R}^n) \neq \mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#McDuff75"}> McDuff 1975, p. 96
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            Still, the basic idea can be salvaged &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"}> Okuyama 2005
            \&amp;rbrack; if only one allows charged points to be replaced by “strings” — concretely: straight line segments of finite lenght in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            each parallel to, say, the first coordinate axis — with charged endpoints, where pair creation/annihilation of strings smoothens out the corresponding interaction of points, in the familiar way in which interactions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            smoothen out singular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            (only that here this is not postulated but emerges in order that the resulting moduli space models
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi Conf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
          %p
            On the right, if a filled circle is taken to indicate that the corresponding point is part of the interval, while an open circle indicates that it is not, then the curved arrows correspond to continuous paths in the configuration space of intervals,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Conf
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^n)
            %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"} Okuyama 2005, Def. 3.1-2
            — there denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_n(S^0)_{\mathbb{R}}
          %p
            With this stringy resolution of the charged points, the expected equivalence does hold &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"}> Okuyama 2005, Thm. 1
            \&amp;rbrack;:
          #eq:OkuyamaTheorem.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Conf
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^n)
                  \\;\;
                  \\simeq
                  \\;\;
                  \\mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^n)
                  \\,.
          %h3#LoopsOfChargedStrings Charged string loops as framed links
          %p
            It follows that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of the configuration space of charged strings in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            is
          #eq:Pi1OfOkuyamaConfigsInThePlane.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Conf
                          %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mtd
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi 𝔾
                        %mi Conf
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 2
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 2
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 3
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{ll}
                  \\pi_1\big(
                  Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)
                  \\big)
                  \&amp;
                  \\simeq\;\;
                  \\pi_1\big(
                  \\mathbb{G}Conf(\mathbb{R}^2)
                  \\big)
                  \\;\;\simeq\;\;
                  \\pi_1\big(
                  \\Omega^2 S^2
                  \\big)
                  \\;\;\simeq\;\;
                  \\pi_0\big(
                  \\Omega(\Omega^2 S^2)
                  \\big)
                  \\;\;\simeq\;\;
                  \\pi_0\big(
                  \\Omega^3 S^2)
                  \\big)
                  \\;\;\simeq\;\;
                  \\pi^3(S^2)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;\simeq\;\;
                  \\mathbb{Z}
                  \\,,
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            where the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            is by Okuyama’s theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:OkuyamaTheorem"} (2)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:SegalTheorem1"} (1)
            and the last one is the statement that the 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group+of+spheres"} homotopy group of
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-sphere"} 2-sphere
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
            generated by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
          %p Hence we ask which loop diagram of charged strings corresponds to the Hopf fibration, under this sequences of isomorphisms.
          %p#Merger
            In the following analysis we consider continuous paths
            %em of
            continuous paths of configurations of charged strings – hence continuous maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Conf
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^2 \to Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChargedStringySaddleMove-240723.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p and the appearance/vanishing of the plain loop diagram.
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChargedStringyVacuumMove-240723.jpg", :width => "560px"}/
          %p#ChargedStringLoopWithTwist
            The following based loop should have a non-trivial class in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Conf
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1\big(Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)\big)
            and we will argue (Thm.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ChargedOpenStringLoopsClassifiedByCrossingNumber"}
            below) that all other based loops have classes which are integral multiples of this one:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChargedStringLoopWithTwist-240717b.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
          %p
            This example brings out the key difference between loops in McDuff’s configuration space of charged points and loops in Okuyama’s configuration spaces of charged strings: The former may essentially be regarded as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+links"} oriented links
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+links"} framed links
          %p
            As such, the above is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unknot"} unknot
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link"} link
            but equipped with non-vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +1
            (say, the choice of the sign is a convention).
          %p
            Its composition with itself, as an element of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+sum"} knot sum
            which is the unknot with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +2
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          #eq:ConnectedSumOfFramedUnknots.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -130px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ConnectedSumOfFramedUnknots-240724.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p#ChargedStringHopfLink
            In view of this, we find that the charged stringy version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+link"} Hopf link
            is equivalent, as an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Conf
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1\big(Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)\big)
            and via the above saddle move, to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unknot"} unknot
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +2
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChargedStringHopfLink-240716.jpg", :width => "320px"}/
          %p
            In fact, by applying the same saddle move on the left, this shows that the Hopf link represents twice the class of the previous example in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Conf
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1\big(Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)\big)
          %p#ChargedStringOppositeHopfLink
            Analogously, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+link"} Hopf link
            with the opposite relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+link"} orientation
            represents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -2
            times that unit class:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChargedStringOppositeHopfLink-240717.jpg", :width => "320px"}/
          %p
            These examples should suffice to illustrate how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+links"} framed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+links"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagrams"} link diagrams
            may be regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loops"} based loops
            in the Okuyama configuration space, and conversely how every based loop of Okuyama configurations – possibly up to some slight continuous deformation – comes from a framed oriented link diagram in this way. Therefore, from now on we just draw
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"} framed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+link"} oriented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagrams"} link diagrams
          %p Our sign convention for crossing numbers shall be
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CrossingSignConvention-240722.jpg", :width => "310px"}/
          %p In this link-diagram notation, the above stringy saddle- and vacuum-move look as shown on the left here:
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          #eq:LinkCobordismMoves.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -100px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SaddleAndVacuumMove-240723.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            These may be recognized as the
            = succeed "/" do
              %em birth
            %em death
            and
            %em fusion
            moves of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "link+cobordism#Khovanov00"} Khovanov 2000, §6.3
            %a{:href => "link+cobordism#Jacobsson04"} Jacobsson 2004, Fig. 15
            and
            %a{:href => "link+cobordism#Lobb24"} Lobb 2024, Fig. 12
            (the latter called
            %em saddle point
            moves by
            %a{:href => "link+cobordism#Kauffman14"} Kauffman 2014, Fig. 16
            and others) which (together with diagram isotopy and the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister+moves"} Reidemeister moves
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+cobordism"} link cobordism
            relation.
          %p
          #VacuumAndSaddleMoveImplyReidemeister.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The vacuum and saddle move imply the
              %a{:href => "framed+link#ReidemeisterMoves"} 1st Reidemeister move
              for
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+links"} framed links
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FramedReidemeisterFromSaddle-240722.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
            %p Therefore, regarding framed oriented link diagrams as loops in the Okuyama configuration space descends to a map
            #eq:TheMap.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FrmdOrntdLnk
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mphantom
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Conf
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    FrmdOrntdLnk \xrightarrow{\phantom{---}}
                    \\pi_1\big(
                    Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)
                    \\big)
            %p
              from (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy+classes"} isotopy classes
              of)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"} framed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+link"} oriented
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/links"} links
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of the Okuyama configuration space.
          %p
          #FramedLinksCobordantToFramedUnknots.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              %strong (Framed links are cobordant to framed unknots)
              %br/
              Every framed link is cobordant
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LinkCobordismMoves"} (5)
              to a framed
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unknot"} unknot
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Via the saddle move
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LinkCobordismMoves"} (5)
              every crossing (in a given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagram"} link diagram
              presentation) of two straight segments may be turned into an avoided crossing of a straight edge and a twisted edge, e.g.:
            %p
              %br/
            #eq:MoveToAvoidCrossing.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (7)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -47px 0px 20px 15px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AvoidedCrossing-240724.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
            %p
              Applying this move to all crossings yields a framed unlink. Further forming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+sum"} connected sum
              of its connected components as in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConnectedSumOfFramedUnknots"} (4)
              finally yields a framed unknot.
          %p
          #ChargedOpenStringLoopsClassifiedByCrossingNumber.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Charged open string loops classified by crossing number)
              %br/
              Under the identification
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Pi1OfOkuyamaConfigsInThePlane"} (3)
              the map
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TheMap"} (6)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi FrmdOrntdLnkDgrms
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo ∼
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mphantom
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Conf
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    FrmdOrntdLnkDgrms_{/\sim} \xrightarrow{\phantom{---}}
                    \\pi_1\big(
                    Conf^I(\mathbb{R}^2)
                    \\big)
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    \\mathbb{Z}
            %p
              is given by sending any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"} framed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriented+link"} oriented
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagram"} link diagram
              to twice its total
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linking+number"} linking number
              plus its
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
              %em all
              the entries in its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linking+matrix"} linking matrix
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By Lem.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FramedLinksCobordantToFramedUnknots"}
              every link maps to the class of a framed unknot. By the above example
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConnectedSumOfFramedUnknots"} (4)
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/UnitFramedUnknotAsHopfGenerator-240724.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
            %p
              Moreover, since the saddle move used in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MoveToAvoidCrossing"} (7)
              manifestly preserves the crossing number, that multiple is the crossing number of the original link.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p Besides the 1st Reidemeister move, the 2nd and 3rd also imply the following moves:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CancellingLeftRightTwists-240724.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
            %p
              Jointly, these moves make the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing+number"} framing number
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+link"} framed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unknot"} unknot
              a well-defined
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagram"} link diagram
              with blackboard framing.
          %p
          #HopfLink.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+link"} Hopf link
              we get
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ±
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm 2
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HopfLinkToFramedUnknot-240722.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
            %p
          %p
          #TrefoilKnot.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trefoil+knot"} trefoil knot
              we get
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ±
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm 3
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TrefoilKnotToFramedUnknot-240722.jpg", :width => "740px"}/
          %p
          #FigureEightKnot.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/figure+eight+knot"} figure eight knot
              we get
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 15px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FigureEightKnotToFramedUnknot-240722a.jpg", :width => "740px"}/
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Under the evident translation between “framing” in the above sense of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+links"} framed links
              and
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+framing"} normal framing
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
              1-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifolds"} submanifolds
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ChargedOpenStringLoopsClassifiedByCrossingNumber"}
              recovers (from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"} Okuyama’s theorem
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+theorem"} Pontrjagin theorem
              in these dimensions (cf.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "Pontryagin's+theorem#Bredon93"} Bredon 1993, p. 126
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Segal73
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Configuration-spaces and iterated loop-spaces
                %strong 21
                (1973) 213-221 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01390197"}> doi:10.1007/BF01390197
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://dodo.pdmi.ras.ru/~topology/books/segal.pdf"} pdf
            %li#McDuff75
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dusa+McDuff"}> Dusa McDuff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Configuration spaces of positive and negative particles
                %strong 14
                1 (1975) 91-107 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(75)90038-5"}> doi:10.1016/0040-9383(75)90038-5
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/81183648.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Okuyama05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shingo+Okuyama"}> Shingo Okuyama
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The space of intervals in a Euclidean space
                %strong 5
                (2005) 1555-1572 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0511645"}> arXiv:math/0511645
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/agt.2005.5.1555"} doi:10.2140/agt.2005.5.1555
          %p The above discussion follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Cohomotopy+and+Framed+Links"} Cohomotopy, Framed Links, and Abelian Anyons
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.11896"}> arXiv:2408.11896
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p After writing this up we learned of the following set of slides, where very similar pictures were already drawn (p. 23-30 and pp. 52):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shingo+Okuyama"}> Shingo Okuyama
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Configuration space of intervals with partially summable labels
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://math.shinshu-u.ac.jp/~ksakai/AGM/18_Okuyama_slide.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Okuyama-IntervalsSlides.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Precursor discussion of the theorem published as
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Okuyama05"} Okuyama 2005
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shingo+Okuyama"}> Shingo Okuyama
                \:
                %em
                  The Space of Intervals and an Approximation to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi n
                        %msup
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi n
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^n \Sigma^n X
                (2003) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1343-7.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Okuyama-Outline.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Okuyama04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shingo+Okuyama"}> Shingo Okuyama
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A simple solution for a group completion problem
                %strong 7
                1 (2004) 69-74 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?repid=rep1&type=pdf&doi=9cdd95b10437b020b32e911934215eb398fbc411"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Okuyama-GroupCompletion.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
